<!--
  🔴 08.09 (F3937): granting GMS the privileged permission WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG.

  The reasoning is functional, not "stock does the same". Measured on the live device: in a single
  boot, PlatformConfigurator from GMS gets
  "java.lang.SecurityException: Permission denial to mutate flag, must have root,
  WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG, or WRITE_ALLOWLISTED_DEVICE_CONFIG" 334 times, and logs
  "setProperties failed with SecurityException" 167 times. So the standard configuration delivery
  mechanism (Phenotype) does not work at all, not "works with warnings".

  Verified that the cause really is the missing grant, not something else:
    - `dumpsys package com.google.android.gms` shows the app DECLARES
      android.permission.WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG;
    - in /product/etc/permissions/privapp-permissions-google-product.xml (a file from MindTheGapps)
      com.google.android.gms is listed with 117 permissions, and WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG is NOT among them
      (grep over the whole file - 0 matches).

  Why a separate file instead of editing the GApps file: Android's privapp-permissions is MERGED from
  every file in the directory, so our addition does not require touching someone else's package and is
  not lost when MindTheGapps is updated.
-->
<permissions>
    <privapp-permissions package="com.google.android.gms">
        <permission name="android.permission.WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG"/>
    </privapp-permissions>
</permissions>
